<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · Lullaby · 1963 · Sub-Saharan Africa</p><h1 class="sg-title">Ee Mungu Nguvu Yetu</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>Kenya's national anthem, written in Swahili in 1963 to an old tune that Pokomo mothers sang to their children.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>the Kenyan Anthem Commission</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional (a Pokomo tune), adapted by the Kenyan Anthem Commission</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Kenya; the Pokomo (the tune)</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swahili</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Kenya</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1963</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem · Lullaby</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>In copyright (words by the Kenyan Anthem Commission (1963), to a traditional Pokomo tune)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ee-mungu-nguvu-yetu">Ee Mungu Nguvu Yetu</a>&quot; (literally &quot;O God, our strength&quot;; in English &quot;O God of all Creation&quot;) is the national anthem of Kenya. It was written in 1963 by the Kenyan Anthem Commission, a body of five members headed by the Kenya Music Adviser, to serve as the state anthem after Kenya's independence from the United Kingdom. It was one of the first national anthems to be specifically commissioned.</p><p>The words were written in Kiswahili, the national language of Kenya, and were meant to express the deepest convictions and highest aspirations of the people. The music is the traditional part: the commission based the anthem on a traditional tune sung by Pokomo mothers to their children.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A prayer to God, as its title says, for the new nation, written to voice the convictions and hopes of the people of independent Kenya. Its mood is solemn and hopeful.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords" data-say>The words of this song are still in copyright, so they are not printed here. &#8220;What it says&#8221; above gives their theme in our own words.</p></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Traditional Pokomo tune sung by mothers to their children</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the anthem's melody is based on it</span></li></ul></section>
